{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_bdrk_01NAMJ6vX9GSNfxDWD4tdRiA","type":"tool_result","content":"=== src/lib.rs is byte-identical to the original translation (no fixes were needed) ===\n9a13a8aa3da37e35fd4cf05871aa1d4e  src/lib.rs\n9a13a8aa3da37e35fd4cf05871aa1d4e  /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-Jyvmhl/tmp/lib.rs.bak\n\n=== Cargo.toml diff (only libloading added) ===\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n\n=== final symbol parity ===\nIDENTICAL: symbol sets match exactly","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d95ce7dd-e3ba-47b2-897b-99e2ab23359d","uuid":"993177f1-1003-492c-82aa-e5485762a369","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:33:39.451Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"=== src/lib.rs is byte-identical to the original translation (no fixes were needed) ===\n9a13a8aa3da37e35fd4cf05871aa1d4e  src/lib.rs\n9a13a8aa3da37e35fd4cf05871aa1d4e  /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-Jyvmhl/tmp/lib.rs.bak\n\n=== Cargo.toml diff (only libloading added) ===\n[dev-dependencies]\nlibloading = \"0.8\"\n\n\n=== final symbol parity ===\nIDENTICAL: symbol sets match exactly","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_ugh2qyicssma3wizxneisdjem5tbkvvelas7xevpcknqomrldf6q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification is complete. **The Rust translation is correct — I found zero behavioural divergences and made no changes to `src/lib.rs`.**\n\n## What the library is\n\nThe entire library is 4 lines of C: one exported function `void driver(double f)` that prints the double's raw bits via a type-punning union (`%llx`), then the value as `%a` and `%.4f`.\n\n## Phase A — surface map (artifacts in the crate root)\n\n| file | finding |\n|---|---|\n| `SYMBOLS.md` | C exports exactly **1** symbol (`driver`); Rust exports the same. **0 missing**, 0 undefined non-libc symbols. Only `src/driver.c` exists, so no module was left untranslated. |\n| `ERRORS.md` | 15 rows. The C error surface is **provably empty** — a comment-stripped, token-based scan finds no `return`/`assert`/`NULL`/`if`/comparison operator. Rows instead cover every degenerate input (sNaN payloads, `-0.0`, subnormal boundaries, one-past-finite-range, rounding cliffs). |\n| `CONFIGS.md` | 28 rows across IEEE-754 class × magnitude regime × sign, **plus two process-state axes I found by inspecting what glibc's `printf` branches on**: FPU rounding direction and `LC_NUMERIC` locale. |\n\n**Build configurations: exactly one.** `Cargo.toml` has no `[features]`; CMake has no `option()`/`-D` variants; the only `#ifdef` is a header guard. `verify.sh` computes the feature powerset mechanically so a future feature is picked up automatically.\n\n## Phases B–D — 53 tests, all passing\n\nBoth `.so`s are always reached through `dlopen`/`dlsym` via `libloading`, never by calling Rust directly, so the `#[no_mangle]` wrapper is under test. ~100k values flow through both sides with a fixed seed. Also re-run under `release` (which sets `panic = \"abort\"`).\n\n## Two vacuity traps I found and closed\n\nThese are the important findings — each would have produced a **fully green suite that verified nothing**:\n\n1. **`cargo test` never relinks a `cdylib`.** Nothing links `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]`, so the tests were loading a stale `.so`: I proved this by changing `%.4f` → `%.5f` and still getting 45/45 passes. The harness now runs `cargo build --lib` before `dlopen` and asserts the `.so` isn't older than `src/lib.rs`.\n2. **Parallel test threads corrupt an fd-1 capture.** libtest's own `test x ... ok` output was being flushed into my capture window by other threads. Fixed with `RUST_TEST_THREADS = \"1\"`, a capture mutex, and a one-line-per-call assertion so residual contamination fails loudly.\n\n## Mutation study — proof the tests can fail\n\n**13/13 mutations caught, 0 survivors** (documented in `VERIFICATION.md`), including NaN-payload quieting, sign-bit loss, one flipped bit, and replacing the `printf` delegation with Rust's own `format!`.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: `(f * 1.0).to_bits()` did *not* fail, but that's **not** a coverage gap — LLVM folds `fmul x, 1.0` to `x`, making the mutant semantically identical. An unambiguous quieting mutation *is* caught by `error_e6`, `error_e7`, `config_c5/c20/c21`. Similarly, \"drop trailing newline\" first looked like a survivor but was actually a compile error from the `&[u8; 14]` length annotation; re-applied correctly, it is caught.\n\n## Why the translation holds up\n\nThe key design decision in the Rust code is that it forwards to the **same `printf@GLIBC_2.2.5`** the C `.so` imports rather than formatting the value itself. That's what makes `%a` hex-floats, `%.4f` round-half-even, `-nan`/`-0.0000` signs, and even non-default rounding modes and locales byte-identical — my C26–C28 rows confirm a self-formatting implementation would have diverged there. `f64::to_bits()` also correctly preserves signaling-NaN payloads, matching the C union pun exactly.\n\nNothing in `c_src/` was modified (checksums verified); the only edit to `Cargo.toml` was adding `libloading` to `[dev-dependencies]`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1057,"cache_read_input_tokens":111181,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1057,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d95ce7dd-e3ba-47b2-897b-99e2ab23359d","uuid":"3efaecbb-b9fa-46b3-8d87-f3e04f9fbb7f","timestamp":"2026-08-18T14:34:00.204Z"}
